= Measurement — Technical =

== Measurement model ==
A measurement result can be modeled through a relation:

<pre>
Y = f(X1, X2, ..., Xn)
</pre>

where <code>Y</code> is the measurand/output quantity and <code>Xi</code> are input quantities.

Each input has an estimate and uncertainty distribution.

Uncertainty in Y follows from the model and input uncertainties.

== Traceability ==
Metrological traceability is a property of a measurement result whereby the result can be related to a reference through a documented unbroken chain of calibrations, each contributing to uncertainty.

Traceability is not simply having an instrument certificate; the full chain and uncertainty contribution matter.

== Calibration ==
Calibration establishes relations of the form:

<pre>
reference quantity value ↔ indication
</pre>

possibly with associated uncertainties.

The resulting calibration function can later transform indications into measured values.

Calibration should be distinguished from:
* adjustment;
* verification;
* validation.

== Type A and Type B evaluation ==
Traditional GUM terminology distinguishes uncertainty evaluation by method:

'''Type A''' — evaluation using statistical analysis of measured quantity values under defined conditions.

'''Type B''' — evaluation using other information, such as calibration certificates, specifications, previous measurements, or scientific judgment.

The distinction concerns evaluation method, not two fundamentally different ontological kinds of uncertainty.

== Propagation ==
For approximately linear models, standard uncertainties can be propagated using sensitivity coefficients.

For nonlinear or non-Gaussian cases, Monte Carlo propagation can represent output distributions more faithfully.

Correlations among inputs must be included.

== Repeatability / reproducibility conditions ==
The VIM defines sets of conditions under which precision is assessed.

Repeatability typically holds factors such as procedure, operator, instrument, location, and short time interval constant.

Reproducibility conditions deliberately change specified factors.

These metrological meanings must be distinguished from computational reproducibility in [[Reproducibility/Technical|Reproducibility — Technical]].

== Measurement and constructs ==
Direct physical measurement differs from measurement of latent constructs.

For constructs such as intelligence, pain, trust, or socioeconomic status, measurement relies heavily on operational definitions, psychometric models, and validation evidence.

Precision of a numerical score cannot compensate for invalid construct representation.

